<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Esch-sur-Alzette</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Esch-sur-Alzette is the second-largest city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luxembourg">Luxembourg</a>, in the south of the country near the French border. It grew from a small town into the centre of the Luxembourg <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/steel">steel</a> industry after the iron ore of the region, the minette, was exploited from the late nineteenth century. Today it hosts the main campus of the University of Luxembourg at Belval, built on the site of a former steelworks.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/esch-sur-alzette.jpg" alt="Esch-sur-Alzette" width="320" height="190" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Zinneke · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:View_of_Esch-sur-Alzette_2025-04_--2_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Esch-sur-Alzette lies in the south-west of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luxembourg">Luxembourg</a>, on the border with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, in the valley of the Alzette, which flows through the city. It is usually called just Esch, and the full name distinguishes it from the village of Esch-sur-Sûre, 45 kilometres further north. Luxembourg City is roughly 15 kilometres to the north-east.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The town was first mentioned on 12 April 1128, in a message to Pope Honorius II. For a long time it was a small farming village in the valley. That changed when important amounts of iron ore were found in the area in the 1850s. With the development of the mines and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/steel">steel</a> industry the population multiplied tenfold in a couple of decades, and the workers came from across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>, making Esch a multicultural community. The steel company <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arbed">ARBED</a> was founded in 1911.</p><p>In the 1970s, as a result of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/steel">steel</a> crisis, the mines and many of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/blast-furnace">blast furnaces</a> were shut down. The last one, at Esch-Belval, stopped operating for good in 1997, and an electric furnace fed with scrap metal replaced the blast furnaces. In 2001 a film company built a huge backdrop on the Terre Rouge, a former steelworks site, a copy of sixteenth-century <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a> with a 600-metre <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/grand-canal">Grand Canal</a>. Films such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/the-merchant-of-venice">The Merchant of Venice</a> were shot there until it was torn down in 2007.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Esch-sur-Alzette is the second most populous commune of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luxembourg">Luxembourg</a>, with a population of about thirty-eight thousand. The industrial wasteland at Belval is being redeveloped as a new town quarter, and the area around the old <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/blast-furnace">blast furnaces</a> hosts structures of the University of Luxembourg, research centres and the national archives. Esch has a conservatory of music and the longest shopping street in Luxembourg. It was a European Capital of Culture for 2022, together with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kaunas">Kaunas</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/novi-sad">Novi Sad</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Esch-sur-Alzette” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The decisive change came from the phosphoric iron ore, the minette, of the south around <b>Esch-sur-Alzette</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/grand-duchy-of-luxembourg">Grand Duchy of Luxembourg · Socioeconomy</a></li></ul></section>
